IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
CASE NO: W.P. (C) No. 1031 of 2019
CITATION: (2020) 3 SCC 637
YEAR: 2020

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Judges: N.V. Ramana, R. Subhash Reddy, B.R. Gavai

PARTIES:
Anuradha Bhasin vs Union of India

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Constitution of India, Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973, Indian Telegraph Act, 1885

KEY PROVISIONS & SECTIONS:
Article 19(1)(a), Article 19(1)(g), Section 144 CrPC

LEGAL KEYWORDS & TOPICS:
internet shutdown, freedom of expression on internet, proportionality, suspension rules

================================================================================
                    JUDGMENT SUMMARY & RATIO DECIDENDI
================================================================================

Held that access to the internet is constitutionally protected under Article 19(1)(a) and 19(1)(g). Indefinite internet suspensions are impermissible and must comply with proportionality and periodic review.

1. ISSUES CONSIDERED:
   The court addressed questions of legal interpretation under Article 19(1)(a), Article 19(1)(g), Section 144 CrPC.

2. ANALYSIS & PRECEDENT CONTEXT:
   Having examined the statutory scheme, fundamental principles of jurisprudence,
   and constitutional safeguards, the Court reiterated the necessity of strict
   compliance with statutory procedure and evidentiary standards.

3. HELD & BINDING RATIO:
   The principle formulated in this judgment constitutes binding judicial precedent
   within its territorial and appellate jurisdiction.

